        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
